LLM-generated summary: This extended conversation between Max More and Dr. Gregory Fahy centers on the landmark ultrastructural analysis of a vitrified human brain from Alcor patient Dr. Stephen Coles, together with parallel rabbit-brain experiments, demonstrating that high-fidelity preservation of synapses, myelinated axons, dendrites, and overall cellular architecture is achievable even after substantial warm and cold ischemia. Fahy explains how partial permeation of the blood-brain barrier by low-molecular-weight cryoprotectants such as M22, combined with osmotic water efflux, enables vitrification without frank ice formation, that membrane osmotic responsiveness survives dehydration, and that fracturing can be avoided down to at least −140 °C inside the skull. The dialogue also covers ongoing work toward functional revival, including hippocampal-slice electrophysiology, organoid learning-and-memory assays after vitrification, new low-toxicity cryoprotectants, and supercooling agents that may extend hypothermic organ banking. Broader implications for the credibility of cryonics within mainstream cryobiology, the relative merits of chemical fixation versus vitrification for connectome and molecular-inventory preservation, and the technological overlap with transplant-organ banking are examined in detail.

[[!toc levels=3]]

# Introduction and Guest Background

Hello and welcome back to the Biostasis Technologies podcast. Today we have, well, we always have special guests, but we have an extra special guest this time, perhaps the world's leading cryobiologist. Dr. Gregory Fahy is Vice President and Chief Scientific Officer of 21st Century Medicine. He basically is the inventor of vitrification. I think that's not too much of an exaggeration to say. He co-founded Intervene Immune, a company developing clinical methods to reverse immune system aging. He was also from 2022 to 2023 president of the Society for Cryobiology. He's also, I consider, a good friend. And I've known Greg for, I'm not sure how many years or decades, but a long time. Welcome, Greg.

A long time. Oh, well, thank you. Thank you for having me, Max.

