# Insights

- Osmotic water efflux plus partial CPA permeation concentrates intracellular solutes sufficiently for vitrification even when the blood-brain barrier remains largely closed.
- Membrane integrity is demonstrated by post-rehydration volume recovery of neurons, proving that dehydration is reversible and not equivalent to chemical fixation.
- Intermediate-temperature storage (−140 °C) prevents fracturing while still allowing indefinite storage below the glass-transition temperature.
- Registry and relocation problems introduced by fractures cannot be assumed to be solved by future molecular nanotechnology because mainstream medicine will not generate the required repair modalities.
- Functional-revival assays (K/Na ratio, hippocampal electrophysiology, trained organoid behavior) provide a more decisive test than static ultrastructure alone.
- New non-protein ice blockers and low-toxicity CPAs discovered by high-throughput screening and AI-assisted design are expected to generalize across organs.

